<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.7.2: Comparing Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01367</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.7.2: Comparing Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e6ef678b-0f79-4950-b9af-97f6118dab2b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>The value of some cell phones changes exponentially after initial release. Here are graphs showing the depreciation of two phones 1, 2, and 3 years after they were released.</para>
<para>Phone A</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of an exponential function, origin O. time (years) and value (dollars)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ed255e46d77de5a8f2270113e81764f77b8780eb"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Phone B</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of an exponential function, origin O. time (years) and value (dollars).  "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/25bf06446555406d7db5441aef3160353edd937f"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Which phone is more expensive to buy when it is first released? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Phone A costs $1,000 and Phone B costs $840, so Phone A is more expensive. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Which graph depicts the value of the cell phone falling in value more quickly? Explain how you know. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Phone A is depreciating more quickly than phone B. This was determined by comparing the value of the phones each year. For instance, after one year, phone A was only worth $600 compared to phone B's value of $630. And, again, after three years, phone A was valued at $216 compared to phone B's value of $354.38.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How does the value of phone A change with every passing year? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Phone A loses $400 in the first year, which is \(\frac25\) (or 0.4 or 40%) of the original value. It loses $240 in the second year, which is \(\frac25\) (or 0.4 or 40%) of $600. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How does the value of phone B change with every passing year? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> Phone B loses $210 in the first year, which is \(\frac14\) (or 0.25 or 25%) of the original value, and $157.50 in the second year, which is \(\frac14\) of $630.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> If the phones continue to depreciate by the same factor each year, what will the value of each phone be 4 years after its initial release?
        <para>Enter your answer for Phone A.</para>
        </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \(\frac{3}{5} \cdot $216= $129.60\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Enter your answer for Phone B.</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(\frac{3}{4} \cdot ($354.38) \approx $265.79\) </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>For each cell phone, write an equation that relates the value of the phone in dollars to the years since release, \(t\). Use \(v\) for the value of Phone A and \(w\) for the value of Phone B.
        <para>Enter your answer for Phone A.</para>
        </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \( v=1,000 \cdot (\frac{3}{5})^t\)  </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Enter your answer for Phone B.</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \( w=840 \cdot (\frac{3}{4})^t\) </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


